<header><p class="st-kicker">chiefdom · Mesoamerica and the Caribbean</p><h1 class="st-title">Cozumel</h1><p class="st-meta">1441 CE – 1544 CE (approx.) · capital Cozumel</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">An island Maya chiefdom off the east coast of Yucatán, home to a pilgrimage shrine of the goddess Ix Chel and the first landfall of Cortés in 1519.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>Cozumel (Cuzamil) was settled for centuries and became an important trading and religious centre in the Postclassic period. After the fall of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/league-of-mayapan">League of Mayapan</a> around 1441 it was an independent chiefdom, with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/san-gervasio">San Gervasio</a> as a main centre.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The island was a major port of call for seagoing traders who carried salt, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cotton">cotton</a>, honey and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cacao">cacao</a> around the peninsula. Pilgrims came from across <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yucatan-peninsula">Yucatán</a> to consult the oracle of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ix-chel">Ix Chel</a>, goddess of childbirth and the moon. Small shrines and coastal structures dotted the island.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/juan-de-grijalva">Grijalva</a> landed in 1518 and Cortés in 1519, smashing idols and recovering the castaway <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jeronimo-de-aguilar">Jerónimo de Aguilar</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/smallpox">Smallpox</a> struck in 1520, and the population plummeted. The island submitted to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/francisco-de-montejo">Montejo</a> in 1527 and was formally under Spanish control by the 1540s.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><div class="st-emblem" role="img" aria-label="chiefdom, 1441 CE – 1544 CE (approx.)"><svg viewBox="0 0 100 100" aria-hidden="true"><ellipse cx="44" cy="56" rx="16" ry="24"/><path d="M44 34 V78"/><path d="M68 20 V84"/><path d="M68 20 L63 30 H73 Z"/></svg><p class="st-emblem-type">chiefdom</p><p class="st-emblem-region">Mesoamerica and the Caribbean</p><div class="st-span" aria-hidden="true"><span class="st-span-bar" style="left:88.38%;width:2.05%"></span></div><div class="st-span-axis" aria-hidden="true"><span>3000 BCE</span><span>1 CE</span><span>2000</span></div></div><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1441 CE – 1544 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/15th-century">15th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/16th-century">16th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>chiefdom</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>feudal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Cozumel</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Naum Pat</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Maya religion</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Yucatec Maya</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/mexico">Mexico</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Cuzamil</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1492&amp;focus=cozumel" aria-label="Cozumel on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="2338 1422 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Cozumel lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M2658 1678L2591 1670M2658 1678L4910 989M2658 1678L2590 1658"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M2591 1670h0M4910 989h0M2590 1658h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M2658 1678h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M2658 1678h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks its recorded location. <a href="/atlas?year=1492&amp;focus=cozumel">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
